// Class 12 Chemistry Unit IX: Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen (Official Syllabus - 06 Marks)
// Comprehensive Deep Reference Book Standard (O.P. Tandon / Pradeep's Standard)

export const c12Chem9HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">Quick Glossary &amp; Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Essential Core Concepts &amp; Key Definitions &bull; Unit IX: Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen (Official Syllabus - 06 Marks)</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">1. Amines &amp; Nitrogen Hybridisation:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Amines are organic derivatives of ammonia (NH<sub>3</sub>) obtained by replacing one, two, or three hydrogen atoms with alkyl or aryl groups. The nitrogen atom is <b>sp<sup>3</sup> hybridised</b> with a trigonal pyramidal geometry and a C-N-H/C-N-C bond angle of &approx; 108&deg; due to unshared lone pair-bond pair repulsion.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">2. Basicity Order of Aliphatic vs Aromatic Amines:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">In aqueous medium, basicity is governed by the combined interplay of inductive (+I), steric, and hydration effects: <b>(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH (2&deg;) &gt; CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg;) &gt; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N (3&deg;) &gt; NH<sub>3</sub></b> for methyl amines; and <b>(C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH (2&deg;) &gt; (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N (3&deg;) &gt; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg;) &gt; NH<sub>3</sub></b> for ethyl amines. Aniline is a far weaker base (pK<sub>b</sub> &approx; 9.38) because the nitrogen lone pair is delocalized into the benzene ring via resonance.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">3. Gabriel Phthalimide Synthesis:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">A specific method for synthesizing pure <b>primary (1&deg;) aliphatic amines</b> by reacting potassium phthalimide with primary alkyl halides followed by alkaline hydrolysis. Aromatic primary amines (aniline) cannot be prepared by this method because aryl halides do not undergo S<sub>N</sub>2 nucleophilic substitution.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">4. Hoffmann Bromamide Degradation:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Treatment of a primary acid amide with bromine in aqueous or ethanolic NaOH yields a <b>primary amine containing ONE LESS carbon atom</b> than the parent amide (R-CONH<sub>2</sub> + Br<sub>2</sub> + 4 NaOH &rarr; R-NH<sub>2</sub> + Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 2 NaBr + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">5. Carbylamine (Isocyanide) Test:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Heating any <b>primary amine (aliphatic or aromatic)</b> with chloroform and alcoholic KOH produces an extremely foul-smelling <b>Isocyanide / Carbylamine (R-NC)</b>. Secondary (2&deg;) and tertiary (3&deg;) amines do not show this reaction.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">6. Hinsberg Test:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Reaction with <b>Benzenesulphonyl chloride (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>Cl)</b>: 1&deg; amines form sulfonamides with acidic N-H (soluble in alkali); 2&deg; amines form sulfonamides without acidic N-H (insoluble in alkali); 3&deg; amines do not react.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">7. Diazotisation &amp; Arenediazonium Salts:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Reaction of primary aromatic amines (aniline) with nitrous acid (NaNO<sub>2</sub> + HCl) at ice-cold temperatures (<b>273 &mdash; 278 K</b>) produces stable <b>Arenediazonium salts (Ar-N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup>)</b>, which serve as versatile synthetic intermediates for preparing aryl halides, cyanides, phenols, and azo dyes.</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 1 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF1744; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF1744; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">1. Nomenclature &amp; Classification of Amines</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Classification: 1&deg;, 2&deg;, 3&deg; Amines and Quaternary Salts</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Amines are classified according to the number of carbon groups attached directly to the nitrogen atom:<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Primary (1&deg;) Amines (-NH<sub>2</sub> group):</b> One alkyl/aryl group attached to nitrogen (e.g. CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>, Methanamine; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>, Aniline).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Secondary (2&deg;) Amines (&gt;NH group):</b> Two alkyl/aryl groups attached to nitrogen (e.g. (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH, N-Methylmethanamine; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NHCH<sub>3</sub>, N-Methylaniline).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Tertiary (3&deg;) Amines (&gt;N- group):</b> Three alkyl/aryl groups attached to nitrogen (e.g. (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N, N,N-Dimethylmethanamine).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Quaternary Ammonium Salts [R<sub>4</sub>N]<sup>+</sup>X<sup>-</sup>:</b> Four alkyl/aryl groups bonded tetrahedrally to nitrogen with a formal positive charge.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. IUPAC Nomenclature Rules &amp; Worked Examples</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Aliphatic amines are named by replacing the terminal &apos;-e&apos; of the parent alkane with <b>&apos;-amine&apos;</b> (Alkanamine). For 2&deg; and 3&deg; amines, the largest alkyl chain is taken as parent, and other groups are specified with prefix <b>&apos;N-&apos;</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Examples:</b><br>\n        1. <b>CH<sub>3</sub>-CH(NH<sub>2</sub>)-CH<sub>3</sub>:</b> Propan-2-amine.<br>\n        2. <b>H<sub>2</sub>N-(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>6</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub>:</b> Hexane-1,6-diamine (Hexamethylenediamine).<br>\n        3. <b>CH<sub>3</sub>-NH-CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>:</b> N-Methylethanamine.<br>\n        4. <b>(CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>2</sub>N-CH<sub>3</sub>:</b> N,N-Diethylethanamine / N-Ethyl-N-methylethanamine.<br>\n        5. <b>p-CH<sub>3</sub>-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub>:</b> 4-Methylaniline (p-Toluidine).<br>\n        6. <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub>:</b> Phenylmethanamine (Benzylamine).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF1744; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF1744; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">2. Structure &amp; Basicity of Amines (Inductive, Steric &amp; Hydration Effects)</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF1744; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">ELECTRONIC STRUCTURE &amp; MULTI-FACTOR BASICITY ANALYSIS</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">1. Geometry &amp; Inversion:</b> Nitrogen is <b>sp<sup>3</sup> hybridised</b> with a pyramidal shape. The unshared electron pair occupies one sp<sup>3</sup> orbital. Amines undergo rapid umbrella-like <b>pyramidal inversion</b> (flipping frequency &approx; 10<sup>11</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>), making simple tertiary amines with three different alkyl groups non-resolvable into enantiomers.<br><br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">2. Basicity in Gaseous Phase (Inductive Effect Only):</b><br>\n      In the gas phase without solvent, basicity strictly follows the electron-releasing (+I) power of alkyl groups:<br>\n      <b>3&deg; Amine &gt; 2&deg; Amine &gt; 1&deg; Amine &gt; NH<sub>3</sub></b>.<br><br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">3. Basicity in Aqueous Medium (Combined 3 Factors):</b><br>\n      In water, basicity is governed by the equilibrium: R-NH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &#8652; R-NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>-</sup>.<br>\n      The stability of substituted ammonium cation depends on three opposing effects:<br>\n      &bull; <b>Inductive Effect (+I):</b> Stabilizes cation: 3&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 1&deg;.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Hydration Effect (H-bonding with water):</b> More H-atoms on nitrogen give greater hydration energy: 1&deg; (3 H-bonds) &gt; 2&deg; (2 H-bonds) &gt; 3&deg; (1 H-bond).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Steric Hindrance:</b> Bulky alkyl groups hinder hydration and proton attack: 1&deg; &lt; 2&deg; &lt; 3&deg;.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Net Result for Methyl Amines:</b> <b>(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH (2&deg;) &gt; CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg;) &gt; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N (3&deg;) &gt; NH<sub>3</sub></b>.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Net Result for Ethyl Amines:</b> <b>(C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH (2&deg;) &gt; (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N (3&deg;) &gt; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg;) &gt; NH<sub>3</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Why is Aniline a much weaker base than Aliphatic Amines?</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      1. <b>Resonance Delocalization:</b> In unprotonated aniline, the lone pair on nitrogen is delocalized over the ortho and para positions of the benzene ring through 5 canonical resonance structures, significantly lowering lone pair availability.<br>\n      2. <b>Anilinium Cation Destabilization:</b> Upon protonation, the anilinium ion (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>) possesses only 2 resonance structures, meaning protonation results in loss of resonance energy.<br>\n      3. <b>Substituent Effects:</b> Electron-donating groups (-OCH<sub>3</sub>, -CH<sub>3</sub>) increase basicity; Electron-withdrawing groups (-NO<sub>2</sub>, -CN, -X) sharply decrease basicity (p-Toluidine &gt; Aniline &gt; p-Nitroaniline).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF1744; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF1744; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">3. Methods of Preparation of Amines</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Reduction of Nitro Compounds, Nitriles &amp; Amides</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Reduction of Nitro Compounds:</b> R-NO<sub>2</sub> + 3 H<sub>2</sub> / Pd &rarr; R-NH<sub>2</sub> + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O. (Laboratory/industrial preference: <b>Fe + conc. HCl</b>, because FeCl<sub>2</sub> hydrolyzes to regenerate HCl, requiring only a catalytic amount of acid).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Reduction of Nitriles (Mendius Reaction):</b> R-C&equiv;N + 2 H<sub>2</sub> / Ni &rarr; <b>R-CH<sub>2</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub></b> (Increases chain length by 1 carbon).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Reduction of Amides:</b> R-CONH<sub>2</sub> + LiAlH<sub>4</sub> &rarr; (H<sub>2</sub>O) &rarr; <b>R-CH<sub>2</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub></b> (Same number of carbon atoms).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Gabriel Phthalimide Synthesis (Pure 1&deg; Aliphatic Amines Only)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Phthalimide is treated with ethanolic KOH to form Potassium phthalimide, which reacts with a 1&deg; alkyl halide via S<sub>N</sub>2 to form N-alkylphthalimide. Subsequent alkaline hydrolysis with aqueous NaOH yields pure <b>Primary Aliphatic Amine</b> and Sodium phthalate.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">CRUCIAL BOARD TRAP:</b> Aromatic primary amines (aniline) <b>cannot be prepared</b> by Gabriel synthesis because aryl halides (chlorobenzene/bromobenzene) do not undergo S<sub>N</sub>2 displacement with phthalimide anion due to partial double bond character of C-X bond and steric repulsion from the &pi;-electron ring.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">3. Hoffmann Bromamide Degradation Reaction</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Heating a primary acid amide with bromine in aqueous or ethanolic NaOH causes decarbonylation, yielding a <b>Primary amine containing ONE LESS carbon atom</b> than the parent amide:<br>\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px 14px; text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; font-size: 15px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">R-CONH<sub>2</sub> + Br<sub>2</sub> + 4 NaOH &rarr; R-NH<sub>2</sub> + Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 2 NaBr + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O</div>\n        <i>(The alkyl/aryl group migrates with retention of configuration from carbonyl carbon to nitrogen via an isocyanate intermediate R-N=C=O).</i>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF1744; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF1744; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">4. Chemical Properties &amp; Diagnostic Distinction Tests of Amines</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Carbylamine Reaction (Isocyanide Test for 1&deg; Amines)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Heating any primary amine with chloroform and alcoholic KOH yields an extremely offensive smelling <b>Carbylamine / Isocyanide (R-NC)</b>:<br>\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px 14px; text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; font-size: 15px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">R-NH<sub>2</sub> + CHCl<sub>3</sub> + 3 KOH(alc.) &rarr; (&Delta;) &rarr; R-NC (Foul smell) + 3 KCl + 3 H<sub>2</sub>O</div>\n        <i>(2&deg; and 3&deg; amines do not give this test &bull; exclusive diagnostic test for 1&deg; amines).</i>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Hinsberg Test for Distinction of 1&deg;, 2&deg;, and 3&deg; Amines</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Reagent: <b>Benzenesulphonyl chloride (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>Cl)</b>.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">1&deg; Amine:</b> Forms N-alkylbenzenesulphonamide (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>NHR). The hydrogen on nitrogen is strongly acidic due to electron-withdrawing -SO<sub>2</sub>- group &rarr; <b>Dissolves in aqueous KOH</b>.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">2&deg; Amine:</b> Forms N,N-dialkylbenzenesulphonamide (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>NR<sub>2</sub>). Has no acidic hydrogen on nitrogen &rarr; <b>Insoluble in aqueous KOH</b>.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">3&deg; Amine:</b> Has no hydrogen attached to nitrogen &rarr; <b>Does not react</b> (remains insoluble in alkali, dissolves in acid).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">3. Reaction with Nitrous Acid (HNO<sub>2</sub>)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">1&deg; Aliphatic Amines:</b> Form unstable alkyldiazonium salts which decompose quantitatively liberating <b>Nitrogen gas (N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;)</b> and a mixture of alcohols, alkenes, and alkyl halides.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">1&deg; Aromatic Amines (Aniline):</b> At 273&ndash;278 K, forms a stable <b>Benzenediazonium salt (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup>)</b> (Diazotisation).<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">2&deg; Amines:</b> Form yellow oily <b>N-Nitrosamines</b> (R<sub>2</sub>N-NO) which give Liebermann nitroso test.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 5 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF1744; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF1744; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">5. Electrophilic Aromatic Substitution in Aniline</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF1744; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">RING ACTIVATION, PROTECTION BY ACETYLATION &amp; ANOMALIES</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">1. Bromination:</b> The -NH<sub>2</sub> group is exceptionally activating (+R effect). Aniline reacts instantly with bromine water without any catalyst to give a white precipitate of <b>2,4,6-Tribromoaniline</b>.<br>\n      <i>To prepare monobromo derivative (4-bromoaniline), the amino group is first protected by acetylation with acetic anhydride/pyridine to acetanilide (reducing activation via amide resonance), brominated, and then hydrolysed.</i><br><br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">2. Nitration (The 47% Meta-Nitration Anomaly):</b> Direct nitration with conc. HNO<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> produces <b>51% para, 47% meta, and 2% ortho-nitroaniline</b>.<br>\n      <i>Reason: In strongly acidic nitrating mixture, aniline is extensively protonated to the <b>Anilinium ion (-NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>)</b>, which is a strongly deactivating meta-directing group! To obtain p-nitroaniline selectively, the -NH<sub>2</sub> group is protected by acetylation before nitration.</i><br><br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">3. Sulphonation &amp; Zwitterion Formation:</b> Heating aniline with conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> at 453&ndash;473 K forms <b>Sulphanilic acid (p-aminobenzenesulphonic acid)</b>, which exists as an internal dipolar <b>Zwitterion (H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-SO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>)</b>.<br><br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">4. Why Aniline Does NOT Undergo Friedel-Crafts Reaction:</b> Aniline is a Lewis base; its amino group forms a coordinate complex with the Lewis acid catalyst anhydrous AlCl<sub>3</sub> (-NH<sub>2</sub> &bull;&bull;&rarr; AlCl<sub>3</sub>), placing a formal positive charge on nitrogen which strongly deactivates the ring.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 6 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF1744; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF1744; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">6. Cyanides (Nitriles) &amp; Isocyanides (Isonitriles)</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Structures &amp; Formation from Alkyl Halides (KCN vs AgCN)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Cyanide ion ([:C&equiv;N:]<sup>-</sup>) is an <b>ambident nucleophile</b> capable of attacking through carbon or nitrogen:<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; With KCN (Ionic):</b> KCN ionizes into K<sup>+</sup> and CN<sup>-</sup>. Attack occurs predominantly through carbon because C-C bond is more stable than C-N bond, yielding <b>Alkyl cyanides (R-CN)</b> as major product.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; With AgCN (Covalent):</b> Ag-C bond is predominantly covalent; only the nitrogen lone pair is available for nucleophilic attack, yielding <b>Alkyl isocyanides (R-NC)</b> as major product.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Comparative Chemical Reactions of Cyanides vs Isocyanides</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Complete Hydrolysis:</b><br>\n        &bull; R-CN + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O + H<sup>+</sup> &rarr; <b>R-COOH (Carboxylic acid)</b> + NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>.<br>\n        &bull; R-NC + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O + H<sup>+</sup> &rarr; <b>R-NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg; Amine)</b> + HCOOH (Formic acid).<br><br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Reduction:</b><br>\n        &bull; R-CN + 4 [H] (LiAlH<sub>4</sub> or Na/EtOH) &rarr; <b>R-CH<sub>2</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg; Amine)</b>.<br>\n        &bull; R-NC + 4 [H] (LiAlH<sub>4</sub> or H<sub>2</sub>/Ni) &rarr; <b>R-NH-CH<sub>3</sub> (Secondary Amine with methyl group)</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 7 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF1744; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF1744; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">7. Diazonium Salts: Preparation, Mechanism &amp; Synthetic Importance</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF1744; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">MECHANISM OF DIAZOTISATION &amp; REACTION ARSENAL</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">1. Preparation &amp; Mechanism (273 &mdash; 278 K):</b><br>\n      NaNO<sub>2</sub> + HCl &rarr; HNO<sub>2</sub> + NaCl. Protonation yields nitrosonium ion (:N=O<sup>+</sup>), which attacks aniline: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + NO<sup>+</sup> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>-NO &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N=N-OH &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup>, -H<sub>2</sub>O) &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> (Benzenediazonium chloride)</b>.<br><br>\n\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">2. Replacement Reactions (Loss of N<sub>2</sub>):</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Sandmeyer Reaction:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + Cu<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>/HCl &rarr; <b>Ar-Cl</b>; Cu<sub>2</sub>Br<sub>2</sub>/HBr &rarr; <b>Ar-Br</b>; CuCN/KCN &rarr; <b>Ar-CN</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Gattermann Reaction:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + Cu powder / HCl &rarr; <b>Ar-Cl</b>; Cu powder / HBr &rarr; <b>Ar-Br</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Iodination:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + KI &rarr; <b>Ar-I</b> + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr; + KCl.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Balz-Schiemann Reaction:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + HBF<sub>4</sub> &rarr; ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>BF<sub>4</sub><sup>-</sup> &rarr; (&Delta;) &rarr; <b>Ar-F (Fluorobenzene)</b> + BF<sub>3</sub> + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Deamination (Replacement by -H):</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (Cu<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; <b>Ar-H (Benzene)</b> + H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>3</sub> + HCl + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr; (or with CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH &rarr; Ar-H + CH<sub>3</sub>CHO).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Hydrolysis to Phenol:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (Warm / 373 K) &rarr; <b>Ar-OH (Phenol)</b> + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr; + HCl.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Nitration:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>BF<sub>4</sub><sup>-</sup> + NaNO<sub>2</sub> / Cu &rarr; (&Delta;) &rarr; <b>Ar-NO<sub>2</sub> (Nitrobenzene)</b> + N<sub>2</sub> + NaBF<sub>4</sub>.<br><br>\n\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">3. Azo Coupling Reactions (Retention of Diazo Group):</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Coupling with Phenol (pH 9-10 / 273-278 K):</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH &rarr; <b>p-Hydroxyazobenzene (Orange Azo Dye)</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Coupling with Aniline (pH 4-5 / 273-278 K):</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> &rarr; <b>p-Aminoazobenzene (Yellow Azo Dye)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 8 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF1744; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF1744; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">8. Master Revision Formula &amp; Named Reactions Cheat Sheet</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">1. Master Named Reactions Matrix:</b>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Gabriel Phthalimide:</b> Phthalimide &rarr; (KOH) &rarr; Potassium phthalimide + 1&deg; R-X &rarr; (aq. NaOH) &rarr; 1&deg; Aliphatic Amine.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Hoffmann Bromamide:</b> R-CONH<sub>2</sub> + Br<sub>2</sub> + 4 NaOH &rarr; R-NH<sub>2</sub> (1 less carbon) + Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Carbylamine Test:</b> 1&deg; Amine + CHCl<sub>3</sub> + 3 alc. KOH &rarr; R-NC (Foul smell) + 3 KCl.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Sandmeyer:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + Cu<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>/HCl &rarr; Ar-Cl; Cu<sub>2</sub>Br<sub>2</sub>/HBr &rarr; Ar-Br; CuCN/KCN &rarr; Ar-CN.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Balz-Schiemann:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + HBF<sub>4</sub> &rarr; ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>BF<sub>4</sub><sup>-</sup> &rarr; (&Delta;) &rarr; Ar-F + BF<sub>3</sub> + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Azo Coupling:</b> Diazonium salt + Phenol (pH 9-10) &rarr; Orange dye; + Aniline (pH 4-5) &rarr; Yellow dye.<br><br>\n\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">2. Diagnostic Distinction Matrix:</b>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Carbylamine Test:</b> 1&deg; Amines (Foul smell of isocyanide) | 2&deg; &amp; 3&deg; Amines (No reaction).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Hinsberg Test:</b> 1&deg; (Soluble in KOH) | 2&deg; (Insoluble in KOH) | 3&deg; (No reaction).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Azo Dye Test:</b> 1&deg; Aromatic amine (Orange/red dye with &beta;-naphthol) | 1&deg; Aliphatic amine (N<sub>2</sub> gas evolution, no dye).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF1744;\">&bull; Basicity Order (Aqueous):</b> (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH &gt; (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N &gt; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> &gt; NH<sub>3</sub> &gt; Aniline.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

export const c12Chem9HtmlSolutions = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF1744; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 21px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Class 12 Chemistry Unit IX: Organic Compounds Containing Nitrogen (06 Marks)\n    </h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Master 100 Board Standard Q&amp;A (Section A: 1-40 VSA &bull; Section B: 41-80 SA &bull; Section C: 81-100 LA Derivations)\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.12); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 30px 0 20px 0; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">Section A: Very Short Answer (VSA) Questions (1 Mark each)</h2>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q1: Define Amines and state the hybridisation of the nitrogen atom in amines.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Amines are organic derivatives of ammonia (NH<sub>3</sub>) wherein one or more hydrogen atoms are replaced by alkyl or aryl groups. The nitrogen atom is <b>sp<sup>3</sup> hybridised</b> with a trigonal pyramidal geometry.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q2: Why is the C-N-H bond angle in methylamine (107.1&deg;) slightly less than the tetrahedral angle (109&deg;28')?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because of the <b>unshared lone pair-bond pair electron repulsion</b> on the nitrogen atom, which compresses the bond angle.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q3: Give the systematic IUPAC name of (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH-NH<sub>2</sub>.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Propan-2-amine</b> (Common name: Isopropylamine).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q4: Give the systematic IUPAC name of (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>2</sub>N-CH<sub>3</sub>.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>N-Ethyl-N-methylethanamine</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q5: Give the systematic IUPAC name of H<sub>2</sub>N-(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>6</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub>.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Hexane-1,6-diamine</b> (Common name: Hexamethylenediamine).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q6: What is pyramidal inversion (umbrella inversion) in amines?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">The rapid umbrella-like flipping of the nitrogen atom and its unshared lone pair through the plane of its three substituents with an oscillation frequency of &approx; 10<sup>11</sup> s<sup>-1</sup>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q7: Arrange 1&deg;, 2&deg;, and 3&deg; methylamines in decreasing order of basic strength in the GASEOUS phase.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N (3&deg;) &gt; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH (2&deg;) &gt; CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg;) &gt; NH<sub>3</sub></b> (governed strictly by the +I inductive effect of methyl groups).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q8: Arrange 1&deg;, 2&deg;, and 3&deg; methylamines in decreasing order of basic strength in AQUEOUS solution.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH (2&deg;) &gt; CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg;) &gt; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N (3&deg;) &gt; NH<sub>3</sub></b> (governed by interplay of +I, hydration, and steric effects).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q9: Arrange 1&deg;, 2&deg;, and 3&deg; ethylamines in decreasing order of basic strength in AQUEOUS solution.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH (2&deg;) &gt; (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N (3&deg;) &gt; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg;) &gt; NH<sub>3</sub></b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q10: Why is Aniline a much weaker base than cyclohexylamine?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">In aniline, the unshared lone pair of electrons on nitrogen is delocalized over the aromatic benzene ring through resonance (+R effect), whereas in cyclohexylamine the lone pair is localized on nitrogen.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q11: Why is Fe + HCl preferred over Sn + HCl for the reduction of nitro compounds to primary amines?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because <b>FeCl<sub>2</sub> formed during the reaction undergoes hydrolysis to liberate HCl</b>, requiring only a catalytic amount of hydrochloric acid to initiate and sustain the reduction.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q12: State Hoffmann Bromamide Degradation reaction with a balanced chemical equation.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>R-CONH<sub>2</sub> + Br<sub>2</sub> + 4 NaOH &rarr; R-NH<sub>2</sub> + Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 2 NaBr + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O</b> (Yields a primary amine with ONE LESS carbon atom).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q13: Name the reactive intermediate formed during the Hoffmann bromamide degradation.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Alkyl isocyanate (R-N=C=O)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q14: Why can aromatic primary amines (e.g. Aniline) NOT be prepared by Gabriel Phthalimide Synthesis?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because aryl halides (chlorobenzene/bromobenzene) do not undergo nucleophilic substitution (S<sub>N</sub>2) with the potassium phthalimide anion under normal conditions due to partial double bond character of C-X bond.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q15: State Carbylamine (Isocyanide) test with chemical equation.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>R-NH<sub>2</sub> + CHCl<sub>3</sub> + 3 KOH(alc.) &rarr; (&Delta;) &rarr; R-NC (Foul smelling isocyanide) + 3 KCl + 3 H<sub>2</sub>O</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q16: Which classes of amines give a positive Carbylamine test?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Only Primary (1&deg;) Amines</b> (both aliphatic and aromatic). Secondary and tertiary amines do not give this test.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q17: What is Hinsberg Reagent? State its IUPAC chemical name.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Benzenesulphonyl chloride (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>Cl)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q18: How does Hinsberg reagent distinguish a primary amine from a secondary amine?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">The product formed with a <b>1&deg; amine (N-alkylbenzenesulphonamide) dissolves in aqueous KOH</b> (due to acidic N-H), whereas the product formed with a <b>2&deg; amine (N,N-dialkylbenzenesulphonamide) is insoluble in aqueous KOH</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q19: What is observed when a primary aliphatic amine reacts with Nitrous acid (HNO<sub>2</sub>)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">It forms an unstable alkyldiazonium salt which decomposes instantly to give a brisk effervescence of <b>Nitrogen gas (N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;)</b> and a mixture of alcohols.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q20: What is Diazotisation? State the required temperature range.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">The conversion of a primary aromatic amine (e.g. Aniline) into an arenediazonium salt using NaNO<sub>2</sub> and HCl at ice-cold temperature of <b>273 &mdash; 278 K (0 &mdash; 5 &deg;C)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q21: Why are Arenediazonium salts stable at 273-278 K while Alkyldiazonium salts are not?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because arenediazonium cations (Ar-N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>) are stabilized by <b>resonance delocalization of the positive charge onto the aromatic benzene ring</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q22: Write the chemical equation for Sandmeyer reaction for preparing Chlorobenzene.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> &rarr; (Cu<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub> / HCl) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl (Chlorobenzene) + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q23: How does Gattermann reaction differ from Sandmeyer reaction?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Sandmeyer reaction uses <b>copper(I) halides (Cu<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>/Cu<sub>2</sub>Br<sub>2</sub>)</b>, whereas Gattermann reaction uses <b>copper metal powder (Cu) with halogen acids (HCl/HBr)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q24: What is Balz-Schiemann reaction? Write the chemical equation.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Reaction of arenediazonium chloride with fluoroboric acid (HBF<sub>4</sub>) to yield diazonium fluoroborate, which upon heating decomposes into aryl fluoride: <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + HBF<sub>4</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>BF<sub>4</sub><sup>-</sup> &rarr; (&Delta;) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>F (Fluorobenzene) + BF<sub>3</sub> + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q25: How is Benzenediazonium chloride converted into Iodobenzene?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">By simply warming the diazonium salt solution with <b>aqueous Potassium Iodide (KI)</b>: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + KI &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>I + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr; + KCl</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q26: How is Benzenediazonium chloride converted into Benzene (Deamination)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">By reduction with <b>Hypophosphorous acid (H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>2</sub>) in water</b>: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (Cu<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>3</sub> + HCl + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;</b> (or by heating with ethanol).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q27: How is Benzenediazonium chloride converted into Phenol?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">By warming the diazonium salt solution with water to <b>373 K (100 &deg;C)</b>: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (373 K) &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH (Phenol) + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr; + HCl</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q28: What is the structure and colour of the dye formed when Benzenediazonium chloride couples with Phenol?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>p-Hydroxyazobenzene (Orange Azo Dye, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N=N-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-OH)</b> at pH 9&ndash;10.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q29: What is the structure and colour of the dye formed when Benzenediazonium chloride couples with Aniline?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>p-Aminoazobenzene (Yellow Azo Dye, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N=N-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub>)</b> at pH 4&ndash;5.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q30: Why does direct nitration of Aniline yield a significant amount (47%) of m-nitroaniline?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because in strongly acidic medium (HNO<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>), aniline is protonated to form the <b>Anilinium ion (-NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>)</b>, which is a powerful electron-withdrawing <b>meta-directing group</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q31: How is the amino group in aniline protected before carrying out monobromination or nitration?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">By <b>acetylation</b> with acetic anhydride in pyridine to form <b>Acetanilide (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NHCOCH<sub>3</sub>)</b>, which decreases the activating power of the -NH<sub>2</sub> group through resonance.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q32: Why does Aniline NOT undergo Friedel-Crafts alkylation or acylation?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because Aniline is a Lewis base and reacts with the Lewis acid catalyst (anhydrous AlCl<sub>3</sub>) to form an insoluble coordinate complex: <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> &bull;&bull;&rarr; AlCl<sub>3</sub></b>, creating a positive charge on nitrogen that strongly deactivates the benzene ring.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q33: What product is formed when Aniline is heated with concentrated H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> at 453-473 K?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Sulphanilic acid (4-Aminobenzenesulphonic acid)</b>, which exists as a dipolar <b>Zwitterion (H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-SO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q34: What is an Ambident Nucleophile? Give an example related to nitrogen compounds.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">A nucleophile that possesses two different donor atoms through which it can attack an electrophile. <b>Cyanide ion ([:C&equiv;N:]<sup>-</sup>)</b> is an ambident nucleophile (attacks through C to form cyanides, or through N to form isocyanides).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q35: Why does Alkyl halide react with KCN to give alkyl cyanide, but with AgCN to give alkyl isocyanide?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>KCN is ionic</b>, releasing free CN<sup>-</sup> where attack occurs via carbon to form more stable C-C bond; <b>AgCN is covalent</b>, where carbon is bonded to Ag and only the nitrogen lone pair is available to attack.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q36: What is Mendius Reaction?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">The reduction of alkyl cyanides (nitriles) with sodium and ethanol (or catalytic hydrogenation with Ni) to yield <b>Primary Amines</b>: R-C&equiv;N + 4 [H] &rarr; (Na / C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH) &rarr; <b>R-CH<sub>2</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub></b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q37: What is formed on complete acidic hydrolysis of: (i) Methyl cyanide, (ii) Methyl isocyanide?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Methyl cyanide:</b> <b>Ethanoic acid + Ammonium ion</b> (CH<sub>3</sub>COOH + NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>).<br><b>(ii) Methyl isocyanide:</b> <b>Methanamine + Formic acid</b> (CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + HCOOH).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q38: What product is obtained upon reduction of Methyl isocyanide with LiAlH<sub>4</sub>?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>N-Methylmethanamine / Dimethylamine ((CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH)</b>, a secondary amine.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q39: Why do primary and secondary amines have higher boiling points than tertiary amines of comparable molecular mass?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because 1&deg; and 2&deg; amines have hydrogen atoms bonded to nitrogen and form <b>intermolecular Hydrogen Bonds</b>, whereas 3&deg; amines lack N-H bonds and cannot form intermolecular H-bonds with each other.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q40: Why are lower aliphatic amines soluble in water?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because they form <b>intermolecular hydrogen bonds with water molecules</b> using their nitrogen lone pairs and N-H hydrogen atoms.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.12); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 30px 0 20px 0; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">Section B: Short Answer (SA) Questions (2 &amp; 3 Marks each)</h2>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q41: Explain why the basicity order of methyl-substituted amines in aqueous solution is 2&deg; &gt; 1&deg; &gt; 3&deg; &gt; NH<sub>3</sub>.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Basicity in water depends on three concurrent factors:<br>1. <b>Inductive effect (+I):</b> More methyl groups stabilize the ammonium cation: 3&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 1&deg;.<br>2. <b>Hydration effect:</b> Greater number of N-H hydrogens allows more H-bonding with water, stabilizing the cation: 1&deg; (3 H-bonds) &gt; 2&deg; (2 H-bonds) &gt; 3&deg; (1 H-bond).<br>3. <b>Steric hindrance:</b> Three methyl groups in 3&deg; amine crowd the nitrogen, hindering hydration and proton approach.<br>The cumulative balance of these three factors makes <b>dimethylamine (2&deg;) the most stable and strongest base</b>, followed by 1&deg;, then 3&deg;.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q42: Why is Aniline a much weaker base than Ammonia? Explain with canonical resonance structures.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">1. In Aniline, the unshared pair of electrons on nitrogen is delocalized over the benzene ring through <b>5 resonance canonical structures</b>, reducing the electron density on nitrogen.<br>2. When aniline accepts a proton to form the <b>Anilinium ion (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>)</b>, it has only <b>2 resonance structures</b>.<br>3. Since unprotonated aniline is more resonance-stabilized than the anilinium ion, protonation is energetically disfavoured, making aniline a weak base (pK<sub>b</sub> = 9.38 vs 4.75 for NH<sub>3</sub>).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q43: How will you convert: (i) Nitrobenzene to Aniline, (ii) Aniline to Acetanilide, (iii) Acetanilide to 4-Bromoaniline?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Nitrobenzene &rarr; Aniline:</b> Reduce with Fe + conc. HCl followed by neutralization with NaOH: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NO<sub>2</sub> + 6 [H] &rarr; (Fe/HCl) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><b>(ii) Aniline &rarr; Acetanilide:</b> Treat with acetic anhydride in pyridine: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + (CH<sub>3</sub>CO)<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (Pyridine) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NHCOCH<sub>3</sub> + CH<sub>3</sub>COOH.<br><b>(iii) Acetanilide &rarr; 4-Bromoaniline:</b> 1. Brominate with Br<sub>2</sub> in acetic acid &rarr; 4-Bromoacetanilide. 2. Acid/base hydrolysis &rarr; <b>4-Bromoaniline</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q44: Explain the complete mechanism and significance of Gabriel Phthalimide Synthesis.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Mechanism:</b><br>1. Phthalimide is deprotonated by ethanolic KOH to form nucleophilic <b>Potassium phthalimide</b>.<br>2. Potassium phthalimide attacks primary alkyl halide (R-X) via an <b>S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanism</b> to form N-alkylphthalimide.<br>3. Alkaline hydrolysis of N-alkylphthalimide with aqueous NaOH cleaves both amide bonds, yielding pure <b>Primary aliphatic amine (R-NH<sub>2</sub>)</b> and sodium phthalate.<br><b>Significance:</b> Produces 100% pure 1&deg; amine without contamination from 2&deg; or 3&deg; amines or quaternary salts.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q45: Distinguish between the following pairs using simple chemical tests: (i) Ethylamine and Diethylamine, (ii) Aniline and Ethylamine.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Ethylamine (1&deg;) and Diethylamine (2&deg;):</b> <b>Carbylamine Test:</b> Ethylamine on heating with CHCl<sub>3</sub> and alc. KOH produces an offensive foul smell of ethyl isocyanide; Diethylamine gives no reaction.<br><b>(ii) Aniline (Aromatic 1&deg;) and Ethylamine (Aliphatic 1&deg;):</b> <b>Azo Dye Test:</b> Aniline reacts with NaNO<sub>2</sub> + HCl at 273&ndash;278 K followed by alkaline &beta;-naphthol to form a brilliant <b>Scarlet/Orange Azo Dye</b>; Ethylamine gives brisk effervescence of N<sub>2</sub> gas with no dye formation.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q46: Explain the Hinsberg Test for distinguishing 1&deg;, 2&deg;, and 3&deg; amines with equations.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">1. <b>1&deg; Amine + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>Cl &rarr; N-Alkylbenzenesulphonamide (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>NHR):</b> The hydrogen on nitrogen is acidic due to the electron-withdrawing sulphonyl group; it <b>dissolves in aqueous KOH</b> to form a clear solution.<br>2. <b>2&deg; Amine + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>Cl &rarr; N,N-Dialkylbenzenesulphonamide (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>NR<sub>2</sub>):</b> Has no acidic hydrogen on nitrogen; remains <b>insoluble in aqueous KOH</b>.<br>3. <b>3&deg; Amine + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>Cl &rarr; No Reaction:</b> Has no hydrogen on nitrogen; remains insoluble in alkali but dissolves in acid.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q47: How will you convert: (i) Ethanamide to Methanamine, (ii) Methanamine to Ethanamine?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Ethanamide &rarr; Methanamine (Step-Down / Degradation):</b> Hoffmann bromamide reaction: CH<sub>3</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub> + Br<sub>2</sub> + 4 NaOH &rarr; <b>CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub></b> + Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 2 NaBr + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><b>(ii) Methanamine &rarr; Ethanamine (Step-Up / Ascent):</b> 1. CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + HNO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>OH. 2. CH<sub>3</sub>OH + PCl<sub>5</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>Cl. 3. CH<sub>3</sub>Cl + KCN &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CN. 4. Reduction with LiAlH<sub>4</sub> &rarr; <b>CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub></b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q48: Explain why direct nitration of aniline gives 47% m-nitroaniline along with 51% p-nitroaniline and 2% o-nitroaniline.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Nitration requires a mixture of concentrated HNO<sub>3</sub> and concentrated H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> (strongly acidic medium). In this strong acid, the basic amino group of aniline gets protonated: <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + H<sup>+</sup> &#8652; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup> (Anilinium ion)</b>. In the anilinium ion, the positive charge on nitrogen exerts a powerful electron-withdrawing (-I) effect, deactivating the ortho and para positions and directing the electrophile (NO<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>) to the <b>meta position</b> (47%). Unprotonated aniline directs to ortho and para positions (51% para).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q49: How can Aniline be converted exclusively into p-Nitroaniline in high yield?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">By <b>protecting the -NH<sub>2</sub> group via acetylation</b>:<br>1. <b>Protection:</b> Aniline + (CH<sub>3</sub>CO)<sub>2</sub>O / Pyridine &rarr; Acetanilide (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NHCOCH<sub>3</sub>).<br>2. <b>Nitration:</b> Acetanilide + conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> / conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> at 288 K &rarr; 4-Nitroacetanilide (major).<br>3. <b>Deprotection (Hydrolysis):</b> 4-Nitroacetanilide + H<sup>+</sup> or OH<sup>-</sup> &rarr; <b>4-Nitroaniline (p-Nitroaniline)</b> + CH<sub>3</sub>COOH.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q50: Explain the structure of Sulphanilic acid and why it exists as a Zwitterion.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Sulphanilic acid possesses both a basic amino group (-NH<sub>2</sub>) and an acidic sulphonic group (-SO<sub>3</sub>H) within the same molecule. The acidic -SO<sub>3</sub>H group transfers a proton to the basic -NH<sub>2</sub> group in an internal acid-base neutralization, forming a dipolar ion known as a <b>Zwitterion: H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-SO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup></b>. Due to this ionic dipolar structure, sulphanilic acid has a high melting point and is insoluble in non-polar organic solvents.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q51: Explain the Sandmeyer reaction and write equations for preparing Chlorobenzene, Bromobenzene, and Benzonitrile from Benzenediazonium chloride.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Benzenediazonium chloride reacts with copper(I) salts dissolved in corresponding halogen acids, replacing the diazonium group with halogen or cyano groups:<br>1. <b>Chlorobenzene:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + Cu<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub> / HCl &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl</b> + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;.<br>2. <b>Bromobenzene:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + Cu<sub>2</sub>Br<sub>2</sub> / HBr &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Br</b> + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;.<br>3. <b>Benzonitrile:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + CuCN / KCN &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CN</b> + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q52: Write chemical equations for the synthesis of Fluorobenzene (Balz-Schiemann) and Iodobenzene from Benzenediazonium chloride.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Fluorobenzene (Balz-Schiemann Reaction):</b> Treat Benzenediazonium chloride with fluoroboric acid (HBF<sub>4</sub>) to precipitate benzenediazonium fluoroborate, then heat the dry salt gently: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + HBF<sub>4</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>BF<sub>4</sub><sup>-</sup> &rarr; (&Delta;) &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>F + BF<sub>3</sub> + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;</b>.<br><b>(ii) Iodobenzene:</b> Warm diazonium chloride with aqueous KI: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + KI &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>I + KCl + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q53: Explain Azo Coupling reaction of Benzenediazonium chloride with: (i) Phenol, (ii) Aniline. Specify the pH conditions and colour of dyes.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) With Phenol (pH 9&ndash;10 / mildly alkaline, 273&ndash;278 K):</b> The diazonium cation acts as a weak electrophile and attacks the electron-rich para position of phenoxide ion to form <b>p-Hydroxyazobenzene (Orange Azo Dye)</b>:<br>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH &rarr; (OH<sup>-</sup>) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N=N-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-OH + Cl<sup>-</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><b>(ii) With Aniline (pH 4&ndash;5 / mildly acidic, 273&ndash;278 K):</b> Couples at the para position of aniline to form <b>p-Aminoazobenzene (Yellow Azo Dye)</b>:<br>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N=N-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub> + HCl.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q54: Why does coupling of diazonium salts with phenol require a mildly alkaline medium (pH 9-10), whereas coupling with aniline requires a mildly acidic medium (pH 4-5)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">1. <b>With Phenol:</b> In alkaline medium (pH 9-10), phenol is converted into the <b>phenoxide ion (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup>)</b>, which is much more reactive and nucleophilic than neutral phenol, facilitating electrophilic attack by the weak diazonium cation.<br>2. <b>With Aniline:</b> In strong acid, aniline is protonated to unreactive anilinium ion; in strong base, diazonium ion converts to unreactive diazotate. Mild acidity (pH 4-5) maintains free neutral aniline while preventing diazonium decomposition.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q55: An organic compound A (C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>7</sub>NO) on treatment with Br<sub>2</sub> and KOH gives B (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>7</sub>N). B on reaction with CHCl<sub>3</sub> and alc. KOH gives an offensive smelling compound C. Identify A, B, C.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Deduction:</b><br>1. Reaction of A with Br<sub>2</sub>/KOH (Hoffmann degradation) yields amine B with 1 less carbon &rarr; A is an <b>Amide (Propanamide, CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>)</b>.<br>2. B (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>7</sub>N) is <b>Ethanamine (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>)</b>.<br>3. B reacts with CHCl<sub>3</sub>/KOH (Carbylamine test) to give foul-smelling isocyanide C &rarr; C is <b>Ethyl isocyanide (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NC)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q56: An aromatic compound A (C<sub>7</sub>H<sub>7</sub>NO) on heating with Br<sub>2</sub>/KOH gives B (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>7</sub>N). B on treatment with NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl at 273-278 K followed by boiling with water gives C (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O). C gives a violet colour with neutral FeCl<sub>3</sub>. Identify A, B, C.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Benzamide (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>)</b>.<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Aniline (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>)</b> (Hoffmann degradation of A).<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>Phenol (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH)</b> (Diazotisation of B followed by hydrolysis with boiling water; gives violet colour with FeCl<sub>3</sub>).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q57: An organic compound A (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NO<sub>2</sub>) on reduction with Sn/HCl gives B (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>7</sub>N). B reacts with HNO<sub>2</sub> to liberate N<sub>2</sub> gas and forms C (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O). C on oxidation with acidified K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub> gives ethanoic acid. Identify A, B, C.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Nitroethane (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NO<sub>2</sub>)</b>.<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Ethanamine (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>)</b> (reduction product).<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>Ethanol (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH)</b> (reacts with HNO<sub>2</sub> liberating N<sub>2</sub>; oxidizes to acetic acid).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q58: Explain the Hoffmann's Ammonolysis of Alkyl Halides and why it is not a suitable method for preparing pure primary amines.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Heating an alkyl halide with concentrated ethanolic ammonia in a sealed tube at 373 K causes nucleophilic substitution: <b>R-X + NH<sub>3</sub> &rarr; R-NH<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (R-X) &rarr; R<sub>2</sub>NH &rarr; (R-X) &rarr; R<sub>3</sub>N &rarr; (R-X) &rarr; [R<sub>4</sub>N]<sup>+</sup>X<sup>-</sup> (Quaternary ammonium salt)</b>.<br><b>Disadvantage:</b> Yields a complex mixture of 1&deg;, 2&deg;, 3&deg; amines and quaternary salt which is extremely difficult to separate industrially. (Using a large excess of NH<sub>3</sub> improves yield of 1&deg; amine).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q59: Why is the boiling point of primary amine (e.g. Butan-1-amine, 78 &deg;C) lower than that of corresponding alcohol (Butan-1-ol, 118 &deg;C)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Both form intermolecular hydrogen bonds. However, <b>Oxygen is more electronegative (3.5) than Nitrogen (3.0)</b>, making the O-H bond more polarized than the N-H bond. Consequently, intermolecular Hydrogen Bonding in alcohols is substantially stronger than in amines, requiring more energy to break.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q60: Why does trimethylamine ((CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N) have a lower boiling point (3 &deg;C) than n-propylamine (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>, 48 &deg;C) despite having identical molecular weight (59)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">n-Propylamine is a primary amine with two N-H hydrogens that form extensive <b>intermolecular Hydrogen Bonds</b>. In contrast, trimethylamine is a tertiary amine with no hydrogen attached to nitrogen; it cannot form intermolecular hydrogen bonds with itself and molecules are held only by weaker dipole-dipole attractions.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q61: Explain the reaction of: (i) Aniline with excess Bromine water, (ii) Aniline with Benzoyl chloride (Schotten-Baumann reaction).\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Aniline with Bromine water:</b> Yields a white precipitate of <b>2,4,6-Tribromoaniline</b> instantly without any catalyst due to super-activation of the ring by -NH<sub>2</sub>.<br><b>(ii) Aniline with Benzoyl chloride:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>COCl &rarr; (aq. NaOH) &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NHCOC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub> (Benzanilide / N-Phenylbenzamide)</b> + NaCl + H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q62: What is the action of Nitrous acid (HNO<sub>2</sub>) on: (i) Diethylamine (2&deg; amine), (ii) Triethylamine (3&deg; amine)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Diethylamine + HNO<sub>2</sub>:</b> Forms a yellow oily liquid of <b>N-Nitrosodiethylamine ((C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>2</sub>N-NO)</b> + H<sub>2</sub>O (gives Liebermann nitroso test).<br><b>(ii) Triethylamine + HNO<sub>2</sub>:</b> Forms a water-soluble salt <b>Triethylammonium nitrite [(C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>3</sub>NH]<sup>+</sup>NO<sub>2</sub><sup>-</sup></b>, which decomposes on warming into diethylnitrosamine and ethanol.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q63: Explain the Liebermann Nitroso Test for Secondary Amines.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">When a secondary amine is treated with HNO<sub>2</sub>, it forms an <b>N-nitrosamine</b>. Warming N-nitrosamine with a crystal of phenol and a few drops of concentrated H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> produces a <b>deep green or blue solution</b>. When diluted with water, the solution turns <b>red</b>, and on adding excess aqueous NaOH, it turns <b>intense royal blue or green</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q64: How will you convert: (i) Nitrobenzene to Benzoic acid, (ii) Aniline to Iodobenzene, (iii) Aniline to Benzonitrile?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Nitrobenzene &rarr; Benzoic acid:</b> 1. Fe/HCl &rarr; Aniline. 2. NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl (273-278 K) &rarr; Diazonium salt. 3. CuCN/KCN (Sandmeyer) &rarr; Benzonitrile. 4. Acid hydrolysis H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> &rarr; <b>Benzoic acid</b>.<br><b>(ii) Aniline &rarr; Iodobenzene:</b> Diazotise with NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl at 273&ndash;278 K, then warm with aqueous KI &rarr; <b>Iodobenzene</b>.<br><b>(iii) Aniline &rarr; Benzonitrile:</b> Diazotise with NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl at 273&ndash;278 K, then react with CuCN/KCN &rarr; <b>Benzonitrile</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q65: How will you convert: (i) Chlorobenzene to p-Nitroaniline, (ii) 4-Nitrotoluene to 2-Bromobenzoic acid?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Chlorobenzene &rarr; p-Nitroaniline:</b> 1. Nitration with conc. HNO<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; 1-Chloro-4-nitrobenzene. 2. Ammonolysis with excess ethanolic NH<sub>3</sub> at 473 K &rarr; <b>p-Nitroaniline</b>.<br><b>(ii) 4-Nitrotoluene &rarr; 2-Bromobenzoic acid:</b> 1. Bromination with Br<sub>2</sub>/FeBr<sub>3</sub> &rarr; 2-Bromo-4-nitrotoluene. 2. Oxidation with alkaline KMnO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; 2-Bromo-4-nitrobenzoic acid. 3. Reduction of -NO<sub>2</sub> to -NH<sub>2</sub> with Fe/HCl. 4. Diazotisation &rarr; 5. Deamination with H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; <b>2-Bromobenzoic acid</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q66: Account for: (i) pK<sub>b</sub> of Aniline (9.38) is higher than that of Methylamine (3.38), (ii) Ethylamine is soluble in water whereas Aniline is insoluble.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) pK<sub>b</sub> Comparison:</b> Higher pK<sub>b</sub> means weaker base. Aniline's lone pair is delocalized into the benzene ring (+R), reducing basicity, whereas the methyl group in methylamine pushes electron density onto nitrogen (+I), increasing basicity.<br><b>(ii) Solubility:</b> Ethylamine has a small alkyl group and forms strong H-bonds with water. In aniline, the large bulky non-polar hydrophobic phenyl ring (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-) overpowers H-bonding, making it insoluble.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q67: Account for: (i) Gabriel phthalimide fails for secondary amines, (ii) Aliphatic diazonium salts cannot be isolated at room temperature.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Gabriel for 2&deg; Amines:</b> N-Alkylphthalimide has only one alkyl group attached to nitrogen; alkaline hydrolysis cleaves both C-N carbonyl bonds yielding primary amines exclusively.<br><b>(ii) Aliphatic Diazonium:</b> Lacks resonance stabilization from an aromatic ring; the aliphatic diazonium cation instantly eliminates N<sub>2</sub> (an excellent leaving group) forming a reactive carbocation.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q68: What is Mustard Oil Reaction (Hofmann's Mustard Oil Reaction) for primary amines?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">When a primary aliphatic amine is heated with <b>Carbon Disulphide (CS<sub>2</sub>)</b> in the presence of mercuric chloride (HgCl<sub>2</sub>), it forms an <b>Alkyl Isothiocyanate (R-N=C=S)</b> which possesses a characteristic pungent odour resembling <b>mustard oil</b>: R-NH<sub>2</sub> + CS<sub>2</sub> + HgCl<sub>2</sub> &rarr; <b>R-N=C=S</b> + HgS&darr; + 2 HCl.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q69: What are Quaternary Ammonium Salts? State their commercial importance as phase-transfer catalysts and detergents.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Tetraalkylammonium salts ([R<sub>4</sub>N]<sup>+</sup>X<sup>-</sup>) where four alkyl groups are attached to nitrogen. <b>Applications:</b> (1) <b>Cationic Detergents &amp; Germicides:</b> Cetyltrimethylammonium bromide ([CH<sub>3</sub>(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>15</sub>N(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>]<sup>+</sup>Br<sup>-</sup>) used in hair conditioners; (2) <b>Phase-Transfer Catalysts (PTC):</b> Tetrabutylammonium chloride transfers ionic reagents from aqueous to organic phase.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q70: How will you distinguish between Methyl cyanide (CH<sub>3</sub>CN) and Methyl isocyanide (CH<sub>3</sub>NC)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Smell:</b> Methyl cyanide has a pleasant ethereal smell; Methyl isocyanide has an extremely foul, obnoxious, repulsive odour.<br><b>2. Reduction with LiAlH<sub>4</sub>:</b> CH<sub>3</sub>CN gives 1&deg; amine (Ethanamine, CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>); CH<sub>3</sub>NC gives 2&deg; amine (Dimethylamine, (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH).<br><b>3. Hydrolysis:</b> CH<sub>3</sub>CN gives Acetic acid + NH<sub>3</sub>; CH<sub>3</sub>NC gives Methanamine + Formic acid.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q71: Write IUPAC names of: (i) Allylamine, (ii) Benzylamine, (iii) o-Toluidine, (iv) N,N-Dimethylaniline.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Allylamine (CH<sub>2</sub>=CH-CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>):</b> Prop-2-en-1-amine.<br><b>(ii) Benzylamine (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>):</b> Phenylmethanamine.<br><b>(iii) o-Toluidine (2-CH<sub>3</sub>-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub>):</b> 2-Methylaniline.<br><b>(iv) N,N-Dimethylaniline (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>):</b> N,N-Dimethylbenzenamine.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q72: Convert: (i) Aniline to Sulphanilic acid, (ii) Aniline to Benzoic acid, (iii) Aniline to Phenol.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Aniline &rarr; Sulphanilic acid:</b> Heat with conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> at 453&ndash;473 K &rarr; Sulphanilic acid (Zwitterion).<br><b>(ii) Aniline &rarr; Benzoic acid:</b> Diazotise (NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl, 273-278 K) &rarr; CuCN/KCN &rarr; Benzonitrile &rarr; (H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; Benzoic acid.<br><b>(iii) Aniline &rarr; Phenol:</b> Diazotise (NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl, 273-278 K) &rarr; Warm with water at 373 K &rarr; Phenol + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr; + HCl.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q73: An organic compound A (C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>9</sub>N) reacts with benzenesulphonyl chloride to give a solid insoluble in alkali. Identify A and write the reaction.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Since the sulfonamide product is <b>insoluble in alkali</b>, Compound A must be a <b>Secondary (2&deg;) Amine</b>.<br><b>Compound A:</b> <b>N-Methylethanamine (Ethylmethylamine, CH<sub>3</sub>-NH-CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>)</b>.<br><b>Reaction:</b> CH<sub>3</sub>NHCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>Cl &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>SO<sub>2</sub>N(CH<sub>3</sub>)(CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>) (N-Ethyl-N-methylbenzenesulphonamide, insoluble in KOH) + HCl.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q74: An organic compound A (C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>11</sub>N) does not react with Hinsberg reagent. Identify A and write its structure.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Since Compound A does not react with benzenesulphonyl chloride, it has no hydrogen on nitrogen &bull; it is a <b>Tertiary (3&deg;) Amine</b>.<br><b>Compound A:</b> <b>N,N-Dimethylethanamine [(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>N-CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>]</b> (or Diethylmethylamine).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q75: Write short notes on: (i) Carbylamine Test, (ii) Hoffmann Bromamide Degradation.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Carbylamine Test:</b> Diagnostic test for primary amines. Heating 1&deg; amine with chloroform and alcoholic KOH yields an intolerable foul-smelling isocyanide (carbylamine). 2&deg; and 3&deg; amines give negative test.<br><b>(ii) Hoffmann Bromamide Degradation:</b> Conversion of an amide into a primary amine with one less carbon atom by treatment with Br<sub>2</sub> and aqueous NaOH via migration of alkyl group to electron-deficient nitrogen in an isocyanate intermediate.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q76: Write short notes on: (i) Gabriel Phthalimide Synthesis, (ii) Balz-Schiemann Reaction.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Gabriel Phthalimide Synthesis:</b> Preparation of pure primary aliphatic amines by S<sub>N</sub>2 alkylation of potassium phthalimide with primary alkyl halides followed by basic hydrolysis.<br><b>(ii) Balz-Schiemann Reaction:</b> Preparation of aryl fluorides by thermal decomposition of dry arenediazonium fluoroborate (ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>BF<sub>4</sub><sup>-</sup> &rarr; Ar-F + BF<sub>3</sub> + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q77: Why is N,N-dimethylaniline an ortho/para directing group in electrophilic substitution?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">The nitrogen atom has an unshared pair of electrons that enters into resonance (+M effect) with the &pi;-system of the benzene ring, increasing electron density preferentially at ortho and para positions.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q78: Explain the preparation of Arenediazonium salt from Aniline with exact reaction conditions.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Aniline is dissolved in dilute hydrochloric acid and cooled in an ice bath to <b>273 &mdash; 278 K (0 &mdash; 5 &deg;C)</b>. A cold aqueous solution of sodium nitrite (NaNO<sub>2</sub>) is added slowly with stirring, keeping the temperature below 278 K to prevent decomposition into phenol: <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + NaNO<sub>2</sub> + 2 HCl &rarr; (273-278 K) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + NaCl + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q79: An organic compound A (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NO<sub>2</sub>) on reduction with Fe/HCl gives B. B on treatment with NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl at 273-278 K gives C. C on reaction with HBF<sub>4</sub> followed by heating with NaNO<sub>2</sub>/Cu gives back A. Identify A, B, C.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Nitrobenzene (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NO<sub>2</sub>)</b>.<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Aniline (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>)</b> (reduction product).<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>Benzenediazonium chloride (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup>)</b>.<br><b>Regeneration of A:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>BF<sub>4</sub><sup>-</sup> + NaNO<sub>2</sub> / Cu &rarr; (&Delta;) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NO<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q80: Compare the basic strengths of Aniline, p-Toluidine, p-Nitroaniline, and N-Methylaniline.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>p-Toluidine &gt; N-Methylaniline &gt; Aniline &gt; p-Nitroaniline</b>.<br><b>Reason:</b> Methyl group in <i>p</i>-toluidine is electron-donating (+I, hyperconjugation), increasing basicity; N-methyl group (+I) increases electron density on nitrogen; -NO<sub>2</sub> in <i>p</i>-nitroaniline is strongly electron-withdrawing (-I, -M), sharply decreasing basicity (pK<sub>b</sub> = 13.0 vs 9.38 for aniline).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.12); border-left: 5px solid #FF1744; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 30px 0 20px 0; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">Section C: Long Answer (LA) Questions &amp; Derivations (5 Marks each)</h2>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q81: Multi-Factor Analysis of Basicity of Aliphatic Amines in Aqueous Solution: Interplay of Inductive, Hydration, and Steric Effects.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Fundamental Equilibrium:</b> R-NH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &#8652; R-NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup> (Substituted Ammonium Cation) + OH<sup>-</sup>. Basicity depends on the relative stability of the conjugate ammonium cation in water.<br><br><b>2. Three Opposing Physical Factors:</b><br>&bull; <b>(a) Inductive Effect (+I):</b> Alkyl groups are electron-releasing; they disperse positive charge on nitrogen in the ammonium cation, stabilizing it: <b>3&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 1&deg; &gt; NH<sub>3</sub></b>.<br>&bull; <b>(b) Hydration Effect (Solvation Energy):</b> Ammonium cations form hydrogen bonds with water molecules. Cations with more N-H protons form more H-bonds, releasing more hydration energy: <b>1&deg; (3 H-bonds) &gt; 2&deg; (2 H-bonds) &gt; 3&deg; (1 H-bond)</b>.<br>&bull; <b>(c) Steric Hindrance:</b> Bulky alkyl groups crowd the nitrogen atom, physically shielding it from hydration by water molecules and impeding proton approach: <b>1&deg; &lt; 2&deg; &lt; 3&deg;</b>.<br><br><b>3. Final Experimental Orders in Aqueous Medium:</b><br>&bull; <b>For Methyl Series (-CH<sub>3</sub>):</b> Hydration and +I effects combine to make 2&deg; highest, and smaller methyl size allows 1&deg; to hydrate better than 3&deg;:<br><b>(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH (2&deg;) &gt; CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg;) &gt; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N (3&deg;) &gt; NH<sub>3</sub></b> (pK<sub>b</sub>: 3.27 &gt; 3.38 &gt; 4.22 &gt; 4.75).<br>&bull; <b>For Ethyl Series (-C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>):</b> Greater +I effect of ethyl group elevates 3&deg; above 1&deg;:<br><b>(C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH (2&deg;) &gt; (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N (3&deg;) &gt; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg;) &gt; NH<sub>3</sub></b> (pK<sub>b</sub>: 3.00 &gt; 3.25 &gt; 3.29 &gt; 4.75).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q82: Complete Step-by-Step Mechanism of Diazotisation of Aniline to form Benzenediazonium Chloride at 273-278 K.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Overall Reaction:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + NaNO<sub>2</sub> + 2 HCl &rarr; (273&ndash;278 K) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + NaCl + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>Step 1: Generation of Active Electrophile (Nitrosonium Ion, :N=O<sup>+</sup>)</b><br>Sodium nitrite reacts with hydrochloric acid: NaNO<sub>2</sub> + HCl &rarr; HNO<sub>2</sub> + NaCl.<br>Nitrous acid is protonated by acid: H-O-N=O + H<sup>+</sup> &#8652; H<sub>2</sub>O<sup>+</sup>-N=O &rarr; (Loss of H<sub>2</sub>O) &rarr; <b>:N=O<sup>+</sup> (Nitrosonium Cation)</b>.<br><br><b>Step 2: Nucleophilic Attack by Aniline on Nitrosonium Ion</b><br>The nitrogen lone pair of aniline attacks :N=O<sup>+</sup>:<br>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub> + :N=O<sup>+</sup> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub>-N=O (N-Nitrosodiphenylammonium ion).<br><br><b>Step 3: Proton Transfer &amp; Tautomerisation (Diazoic Acid Intermediate)</b><br>Loss of proton yields N-nitrosoaniline, which undergoes rapid prototropic shift:<br>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-NH-N=O &#8652; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N=N-OH (Diazohydroxide).<br><br><b>Step 4: Protonation and Dehydration to Diazonium Cation</b><br>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N=N-OH + H<sup>+</sup> &#8652; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N=N-O<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (Loss of H<sub>2</sub>O) &rarr; <b>[C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N&equiv;N:]<sup>+</sup> &#8654; [C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N<sup>+</sup>=N:] (Benzenediazonium Cation)</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q83: Complete Step-by-Step Mechanism of Hoffmann Bromamide Degradation Reaction.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Overall Reaction:</b> R-CONH<sub>2</sub> + Br<sub>2</sub> + 4 NaOH &rarr; R-NH<sub>2</sub> + Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + 2 NaBr + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>Step 1: Base Deprotonation &amp; N-Bromination (N-Bromoamide Formation)</b><br>Base (OH<sup>-</sup>) abstracts an acidic N-H proton from amide, and the resulting conjugate anion attacks Br<sub>2</sub>:<br>R-CO-NH<sub>2</sub> + OH<sup>-</sup> &#8652; R-CO-N<sup>-</sup>H + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (Br<sub>2</sub>) &rarr; <b>R-CO-NH-Br (N-Bromoamide)</b> + Br<sup>-</sup>.<br><br><b>Step 2: Formation of Nitrene Precursor / Bromamide Anion</b><br>Second OH<sup>-</sup> removes the remaining proton on nitrogen: R-CO-NH-Br + OH<sup>-</sup> &#8652; <b>[R-CO-N<sup>-</sup>-Br]</b> + H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>Step 3: 1,2-Alkyl Shift / Intramolecular Rearrangement (RDS)</b><br>Simultaneous departure of bromide ion (Br<sup>-</sup>) triggers the migration of alkyl group R with its bonding electron pair from carbonyl carbon to nitrogen (concerted rearrangement with retention of configuration):<br>[R-CO-N<sup>-</sup>-Br] &rarr; (Slow, RDS) &rarr; <b>R-N=C=O (Alkyl Isocyanate)</b> + Br<sup>-</sup>.<br><br><b>Step 4: Hydrolysis of Alkyl Isocyanate to Primary Amine</b><br>R-N=C=O + 2 OH<sup>-</sup> &rarr; [R-NH-COO<sup>-</sup>] (Carbamate ion) &rarr; <b>R-NH<sub>2</sub> (Primary Amine)</b> + CO<sub>3</sub><sup>2-</sup>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q84: Complete Step-by-Step Mechanism of Gabriel Phthalimide Synthesis for Pure Primary Aliphatic Amines.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Step 1: Acid-Base Formation of Potassium Phthalimide</b><br>Phthalimide has an acidic imide hydrogen (pK<sub>a</sub> &approx; 8.3) flanked by two electron-withdrawing carbonyl groups. Reaction with ethanolic KOH deprotonates nitrogen:<br>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>(CO)<sub>2</sub>NH + KOH &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>(CO)<sub>2</sub>N<sup>-</sup>K<sup>+</sup> (Potassium Phthalimide)</b> + H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>Step 2: Nucleophilic S<sub>N</sub>2 Alkylation</b><br>The phthalimide anion attacks a primary alkyl halide (R-X) via backside S<sub>N</sub>2 displacement:<br>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>(CO)<sub>2</sub>N<sup>-</sup> + R-CH<sub>2</sub>-X &rarr; (S<sub>N</sub>2) &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>(CO)<sub>2</sub>N-CH<sub>2</sub>-R (N-Alkylphthalimide)</b> + X<sup>-</sup>.<br><br><b>Step 3: Alkaline Hydrolysis (Cleavage of Imide Ring)</b><br>Refluxing N-alkylphthalimide with aqueous NaOH cleaves both carbonyl-nitrogen bonds:<br>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>(CO)<sub>2</sub>N-R + 2 NaOH &rarr; <b>R-NH<sub>2</sub> (Pure 1&deg; Aliphatic Amine)</b> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>(COONa)<sub>2</sub> (Sodium Phthalate).<br><br><b>Why Gabriel synthesis fails for Aniline:</b> Chlorobenzene/Bromobenzene cannot undergo S<sub>N</sub>2 displacement because the C<sub>sp<sup>2</sup></sub>-X bond has partial double bond character due to resonance with the benzene ring.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q85: Electrophilic Aromatic Substitution in Aniline: Bromination, Nitration, Sulphonation, and Ring Protection Strategies.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Super-Activation of Ring:</b> The amino group (-NH<sub>2</sub>) donates its lone pair powerfully into the benzene ring via resonance (+R effect), massively increasing electron density at ortho and para positions.<br><br><b>2. Bromination:</b><br>&bull; <b>Direct:</b> Aniline + 3 Br<sub>2</sub>(aq) &rarr; <b>2,4,6-Tribromoaniline (White ppt)</b> + 3 HBr.<br>&bull; <b>Monobromination (via Protection):</b> Aniline &rarr; ((CH<sub>3</sub>CO)<sub>2</sub>O / Pyridine) &rarr; Acetanilide &rarr; (Br<sub>2</sub> / CH<sub>3</sub>COOH) &rarr; 4-Bromoacetanilide &rarr; (H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; <b>4-Bromoaniline (p-bromoaniline)</b>.<br><br><b>3. Nitration &amp; The Meta Anomaly:</b><br>&bull; Direct nitration with conc. HNO<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> gives <b>51% para, 47% meta, and 2% ortho-nitroaniline</b> because acidic medium protonates aniline to meta-directing anilinium ion (-NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup>).<br>&bull; Mononitration: Acetanilide &rarr; (HNO<sub>3</sub> / H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, 288 K) &rarr; 4-Nitroacetanilide &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup>/H<sub>2</sub>O) &rarr; <b>4-Nitroaniline</b>.<br><br><b>4. Sulphonation &amp; Zwitterion:</b><br>Aniline + conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; Anilinium hydrogen sulphate &rarr; (453&ndash;473 K) &rarr; <b>Sulphanilic acid [H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-SO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup> Zwitterion]</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q86: Synthetic Importance of Arenediazonium Salts: Master Roadmap of Transformations into 8 Functional Derivatives.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">Arenediazonium salts (Ar-N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup>) serve as the supreme synthetic gateway in aromatic chemistry:<br><br><b>1. Replacement by Halogens:</b><br>&bull; <b>Aryl Chloride:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + Cu<sub>2</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub> / HCl &rarr; <b>Ar-Cl</b> (Sandmeyer).<br>&bull; <b>Aryl Bromide:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + Cu<sub>2</sub>Br<sub>2</sub> / HBr &rarr; <b>Ar-Br</b> (Sandmeyer).<br>&bull; <b>Aryl Iodide:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + KI &rarr; <b>Ar-I</b> (Iodination).<br>&bull; <b>Aryl Fluoride:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + HBF<sub>4</sub> &rarr; ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>BF<sub>4</sub><sup>-</sup> &rarr; (&Delta;) &rarr; <b>Ar-F</b> (Balz-Schiemann).<br><br><b>2. Replacement by Other Groups:</b><br>&bull; <b>Cyanide / Nitrile:</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + CuCN / KCN &rarr; <b>Ar-CN</b> (Sandmeyer).<br>&bull; <b>Hydroxyl (Phenol):</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (Warm / 373 K) &rarr; <b>Ar-OH</b>.<br>&bull; <b>Nitro (Nitrobenzene):</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>BF<sub>4</sub><sup>-</sup> + NaNO<sub>2</sub> / Cu &rarr; (&Delta;) &rarr; <b>Ar-NO<sub>2</sub></b>.<br>&bull; <b>Hydrogen (Deamination):</b> ArN<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (Cu<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; <b>Ar-H</b> (Benzene).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q87: Cyanides (Nitriles) vs Isocyanides (Isonitriles): Structural Comparison, Ambident Mechanism, and Complete Reaction Arsenal.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Structural Comparison:</b><br>&bull; <b>Cyanides (R-C&equiv;N):</b> Alkyl group bonded to carbon; linear C-C&equiv;N bond; polar; pleasant ethereal odour; higher boiling points.<br>&bull; <b>Isocyanides (R-N<sup>+</sup>&equiv;C<sup>-</sup> / R-N=C):</b> Alkyl group bonded to nitrogen; coordinate covalent triple bond; extremely foul, obnoxious, toxic odour; lower boiling points.<br><br><b>2. Ambident Nucleophile Alkylation:</b><br>&bull; <b>R-X + KCN (Ionic):</b> Ionizes into K<sup>+</sup> and [:C&equiv;N:]<sup>-</sup>. Attack occurs via carbon because C-C bond is more thermodynamically stable than C-N bond &rarr; <b>Alkyl cyanide (R-CN) major</b>.<br>&bull; <b>R-X + AgCN (Covalent):</b> Ag-C bond is covalent; carbon lone pair is unavailable. Attack occurs via the unshared pair on nitrogen &rarr; <b>Alkyl isocyanide (R-NC) major</b>.<br><br><b>3. Comparative Chemical Reactions:</b><br>&bull; <b>Complete Hydrolysis:</b> R-CN + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O + H<sup>+</sup> &rarr; <b>R-COOH + NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup></b>; R-NC + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O + H<sup>+</sup> &rarr; <b>R-NH<sub>2</sub> + HCOOH</b>.<br>&bull; <b>Reduction:</b> R-CN + LiAlH<sub>4</sub> &rarr; <b>R-CH<sub>2</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub> (1&deg; amine)</b>; R-NC + LiAlH<sub>4</sub> &rarr; <b>R-NH-CH<sub>3</sub> (2&deg; amine)</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q88: Master Diagnostic Distinction Scheme: Comprehensive chemical discrimination of all types of Amines and Nitrogen Compounds.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. 1&deg; vs 2&deg; vs 3&deg; Amines (Hinsberg Test):</b><br>&bull; <b>1&deg; Amine:</b> Forms N-alkylbenzenesulphonamide which <b>dissolves in aqueous KOH</b>.<br>&bull; <b>2&deg; Amine:</b> Forms N,N-dialkylbenzenesulphonamide which is <b>insoluble in aqueous KOH</b>.<br>&bull; <b>3&deg; Amine:</b> Does <b>not react</b> with benzenesulphonyl chloride.<br><br><b>2. 1&deg; Amines vs 2&deg;/3&deg; Amines (Carbylamine Test):</b><br>&bull; Only 1&deg; amines give an intolerable foul smell of isocyanide on heating with CHCl<sub>3</sub>/alc. KOH.<br><br><b>3. 1&deg; Aliphatic Amine vs 1&deg; Aromatic Amine (Aniline):</b><br>&bull; <b>Azo Dye Test:</b> Aniline forms a brilliant orange/red dye with NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl followed by alkaline &beta;-naphthol; aliphatic 1&deg; amines give brisk effervescence of N<sub>2</sub> gas with no dye formation.<br><br><b>4. Secondary Amines (Liebermann Nitroso Test):</b><br>&bull; Forms N-nitrosamine with HNO<sub>2</sub> which gives deep blue/green colour with phenol + conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>, turning red on dilution and blue on adding NaOH.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q89: Master Chemical Conversions Series 1: (i) Nitrobenzene to Benzoic acid, (ii) Benzene to m-Bromophenol, (iii) Aniline to 2,4,6-Tribromophenol, (iv) Methyl cyanide to Ethanamine, (v) Ethanamine to Methanamine.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Nitrobenzene &rarr; Benzoic acid:</b> 1. Fe/HCl &rarr; Aniline. 2. NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl (273-278 K) &rarr; Diazonium salt. 3. CuCN/KCN &rarr; Benzonitrile. 4. H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> &rarr; <b>Benzoic acid</b>.<br><b>(ii) Benzene &rarr; m-Bromophenol:</b> 1. Nitration with HNO<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; Nitrobenzene. 2. Bromination with Br<sub>2</sub>/FeBr<sub>3</sub> &rarr; m-Bromonitrobenzene. 3. Reduction with Fe/HCl &rarr; m-Bromoaniline. 4. Diazotisation &rarr; 5. Warm with water &rarr; <b>m-Bromophenol</b>.<br><b>(iii) Aniline &rarr; 2,4,6-Tribromophenol:</b> 1. Bromine water &rarr; 2,4,6-Tribromoaniline. 2. Diazotisation with NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl at 273-278 K &rarr; 2,4,6-Tribromobenzenediazonium chloride. 3. Warm with water at 373 K &rarr; <b>2,4,6-Tribromophenol</b>.<br><b>(iv) Methyl cyanide &rarr; Ethanamine:</b> Reduction with LiAlH<sub>4</sub> in dry ether (or Na/ethanol Mendius reaction): CH<sub>3</sub>CN + 4 [H] &rarr; <b>CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub></b>.<br><b>(v) Ethanamine &rarr; Methanamine (Step-Down):</b> 1. CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + HNO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH. 2. Acidified KMnO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>COOH. 3. NH<sub>3</sub> / &Delta; &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>. 4. Hoffmann bromamide (Br<sub>2</sub>/NaOH) &rarr; <b>CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub></b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q90: Master Chemical Conversions Series 2: (i) Methanamine to Ethanamine, (ii) Aniline to p-Nitroaniline, (iii) Aniline to Fluorobenzene, (iv) Aniline to Benzene, (v) Benzoic acid to Aniline.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Methanamine &rarr; Ethanamine (Step-Up):</b> 1. CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + HNO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>OH. 2. PCl<sub>5</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>Cl. 3. KCN &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CN. 4. LiAlH<sub>4</sub> &rarr; <b>CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub></b>.<br><b>(ii) Aniline &rarr; p-Nitroaniline:</b> 1. (CH<sub>3</sub>CO)<sub>2</sub>O/Pyridine &rarr; Acetanilide. 2. Conc. HNO<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; 4-Nitroacetanilide. 3. H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> &rarr; <b>p-Nitroaniline</b>.<br><b>(iii) Aniline &rarr; Fluorobenzene:</b> 1. NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl (273-278 K) &rarr; Benzenediazonium chloride. 2. HBF<sub>4</sub> &rarr; Benzenediazonium fluoroborate &rarr; (&Delta;) &rarr; <b>Fluorobenzene (Balz-Schiemann)</b>.<br><b>(iv) Aniline &rarr; Benzene (Deamination):</b> 1. Diazotisation with NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl (273-278 K) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup>. 2. Reduction with H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; <b>Benzene</b>.<br><b>(v) Benzoic acid &rarr; Aniline:</b> 1. C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>COOH + NH<sub>3</sub> / &Delta; &rarr; Benzamide (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>). 2. Hoffmann bromamide with Br<sub>2</sub>/4 NaOH &rarr; <b>Aniline (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>)</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q91: Multi-Step Roadmap 1: An aliphatic compound A (C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>7</sub>NO) on reaction with Br<sub>2</sub>/KOH gives B (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>7</sub>N). B on reaction with HNO<sub>2</sub> gives C (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O) with evolution of gas. C on oxidation with PCC gives D (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>O), which gives a positive Iodoform test. Identify A to D.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Propanamide (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>)</b>.<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Ethanamine (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>)</b> (Hoffmann degradation product).<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>Ethanol (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH)</b> (reaction with HNO<sub>2</sub>, liberates N<sub>2</sub> gas).<br><b>Compound D:</b> <b>Ethanal (Acetaldehyde, CH<sub>3</sub>CHO)</b> (PCC oxidation product, gives yellow CHI<sub>3</sub> ppt with I<sub>2</sub>/NaOH).<br><b>Reactions:</b> CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub> + Br<sub>2</sub>/KOH &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (HNO<sub>2</sub>) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr; &rarr; (PCC) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CHO &rarr; (I<sub>2</sub>/NaOH) &rarr; CHI<sub>3</sub>&darr;.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q92: Multi-Step Roadmap 2: An aromatic compound A (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>7</sub>N) on reaction with NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl at 273-278 K forms B (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub>Cl). B on treatment with CuCN gives C (C<sub>7</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N). C on acidic hydrolysis yields D (C<sub>7</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O<sub>2</sub>). D on reaction with SOCl<sub>2</sub> followed by NH<sub>3</sub> gives E. E on treatment with Br<sub>2</sub>/KOH gives back A. Identify A to E.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Aniline (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>)</b>.<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Benzenediazonium chloride (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup>)</b>.<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>Benzonitrile (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CN)</b> (Sandmeyer reaction with CuCN).<br><b>Compound D:</b> <b>Benzoic acid (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>COOH)</b> (acid hydrolysis of C).<br><b>Compound E:</b> <b>Benzamide (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>)</b> (SOCl<sub>2</sub> gives benzoyl chloride then NH<sub>3</sub> gives benzamide).<br><b>Regeneration of A:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub> + Br<sub>2</sub>/KOH &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q93: Multi-Step Roadmap 3: Primary amine A (C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>9</sub>N) reacts with Hinsberg reagent to give a compound soluble in KOH. A reacts with HNO<sub>2</sub> to form B (C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O), which does not give Iodoform test. B on oxidation with CrO<sub>3</sub> gives C (C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O<sub>2</sub>). Identify A, B, C.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">Since A gives a Hinsberg product soluble in KOH, A is a 1&deg; amine. Since B does NOT give Iodoform test, B is a 1&deg; alcohol (Propan-1-ol) rather than propan-2-ol.<br><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Propan-1-amine (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>)</b>.<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Propan-1-ol (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH)</b>.<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>Propanoic acid (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COOH)</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q94: Multi-Step Roadmap 4: An organic compound A (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>3</sub>N) on reduction with LiAlH<sub>4</sub> gives B (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>7</sub>N). B on treatment with CHCl<sub>3</sub> and alc. KOH gives C (C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N) with a foul smell. C on catalytic reduction gives D (C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>9</sub>N). Identify A, B, C, D.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Acetonitrile (Ethanenitrile, CH<sub>3</sub>CN)</b>.<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Ethanamine (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>)</b>.<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>Ethyl isocyanide (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NC)</b> (Carbylamine reaction product).<br><b>Compound D:</b> <b>N-Methylethanamine / Ethylmethylamine (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NHCH<sub>3</sub>)</b> (reduction of isocyanide yields 2&deg; amine).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q95: Structure, Preparation, and Physical Properties of Isocyanides (Isonitriles) and their physiological hazards.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Structure &amp; Dipole Moment:</b> Isocyanides (R-N<sup>+</sup>&equiv;C<sup>-</sup>) contain a divalent carbon with an unshared lone pair and a formal negative charge, while nitrogen bears a formal positive charge. They have dipole moments of &approx; 3.0 D.<br><br><b>2. Preparation Methods:</b><br>&bull; <b>Alkyl halide + AgCN:</b> R-X + AgCN &rarr; <b>R-NC</b> + AgX.<br>&bull; <b>Carbylamine Reaction:</b> R-NH<sub>2</sub> + CHCl<sub>3</sub> + 3 KOH &rarr; <b>R-NC</b> + 3 KCl + 3 H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>3. Physical Properties:</b> Colourless, volatile liquids with lower boiling points than isomeric cyanides due to weaker dipole-dipole interactions; virtually insoluble in water; extremely poisonous and possess an intolerable repulsive odor capable of producing headaches and nausea.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q96: Mechanism of Nucleophilic Attack on Nitriles: Hydrolysis (Acidic vs Alkaline) and Grignard Addition.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Acidic Hydrolysis of Nitriles:</b><br>1. Nitrogen of R-C&equiv;N is protonated: R-C&equiv;N<sup>+</sup>-H &#8654; R-C<sup>+</sup>=NH.<br>2. Water attacks carbocation &rarr; [R-C(OH)=NH] (Imidic acid intermediate) &rarr; tautomerises to <b>Amide (R-CONH<sub>2</sub>)</b>.<br>3. Further hydrolysis of amide yields <b>Carboxylic acid (R-COOH) + NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup></b>.<br><br><b>2. Reaction with Grignard Reagent:</b><br>R-C&equiv;N + R'-MgX &rarr; [R-C(R')=N-MgX] (Imine salt intermediate) &rarr; (H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; <b>Ketone (R-CO-R') + NH<sub>3</sub> + Mg(OH)X</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q97: Detailed Mechanism of Schotten-Baumann Acylation and Benzoylation of Amines with Stereochemical Considerations.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Acylation Mechanism:</b><br>&bull; <b>Step 1:</b> The unshared lone pair of nitrogen in 1&deg; or 2&deg; amine attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of acyl chloride (R'COCl) to form a tetrahedral alkoxide intermediate.<br>&bull; <b>Step 2:</b> Expulsion of chloride ion (Cl<sup>-</sup>) followed by loss of proton yields <b>N-Substituted Amide</b>.<br>&bull; <b>Role of Base:</b> Pyridine (or aq. NaOH) is added to remove the HCl formed, shifting equilibrium forward.<br><br><b>2. Benzoylation (Schotten-Baumann Reaction):</b><br>Reaction of amines or phenols with benzoyl chloride (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>COCl) in presence of aqueous NaOH: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>COCl + NaOH &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NHCOC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub> (Benzanilide)</b> + NaCl + H<sub>2</sub>O.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q98: Quantitative Basicity Constants (pK<sub>b</sub> &amp; K<sub>b</sub>) and Comprehensive Quantitative Analysis across Aliphatic, Alicyclic, and Aromatic Amines.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Quantitative pK<sub>b</sub> Scale (Lower pK<sub>b</sub> = Stronger Base):</b><br>&bull; Diethylamine ((C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH): <b>pK<sub>b</sub> = 3.00</b>.<br>&bull; Dimethylamine ((CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>NH): <b>pK<sub>b</sub> = 3.27</b>.<br>&bull; Triethylamine ((C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N): <b>pK<sub>b</sub> = 3.25</b>.<br>&bull; Ethylamine (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>): <b>pK<sub>b</sub> = 3.29</b>.<br>&bull; Methylamine (CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>): <b>pK<sub>b</sub> = 3.38</b>.<br>&bull; Trimethylamine ((CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>N): <b>pK<sub>b</sub> = 4.22</b>.<br>&bull; Ammonia (NH<sub>3</sub>): <b>pK<sub>b</sub> = 4.75</b>.<br>&bull; p-Toluidine (4-CH<sub>3</sub>-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>): <b>pK<sub>b</sub> = 8.88</b>.<br>&bull; Aniline (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>): <b>pK<sub>b</sub> = 9.38</b>.<br>&bull; p-Nitroaniline (4-NO<sub>2</sub>-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>): <b>pK<sub>b</sub> = 13.00</b>.<br><br><b>2. Conclusions:</b> Aliphatic secondary amines are the strongest bases in water; aromatic amines are ~1 million times weaker than aliphatic amines due to resonance delocalization.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q99: Industrial Synthesis, Properties, and High-Tech Applications of Hexamethylenediamine and Sulphanilic acid.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Hexamethylenediamine [H<sub>2</sub>N-(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>6</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub>, Hexane-1,6-diamine]:</b><br>&bull; <b>Manufacture:</b> Catalytic hydrogenation of Adiponitrile: NC-(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>4</sub>-CN + 4 H<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (Ni / 373 K) &rarr; <b>H<sub>2</sub>N-(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>6</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub></b>.<br>&bull; <b>High-Tech Application:</b> Condensation polymerisation with Adipic acid at 553 K under pressure yields <b>Nylon-6,6</b> (high-strength synthetic polymer used in textiles, tire cords, and ropes).<br><br><b>2. Sulphanilic Acid (4-Aminobenzenesulphonic acid):</b><br>&bull; <b>Manufacture:</b> Heating aniline with conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> at 453-473 K.<br>&bull; <b>Structure:</b> Exists as internal Zwitterion (H<sub>3</sub>N<sup>+</sup>-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-SO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>).<br>&bull; <b>Applications:</b> Master starting material for synthesizing antibacterial <b>Sulpha Drugs (e.g. Sulphanilamide, Sulphadiazine)</b> and Methyl Orange indicator.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 23, 68, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF1744; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q100: Synthetic Organic Roadmap: Comprehensive interconnected web of transformations between Alkanes, Alkyl Halides, Alcohols, Carbonyls, Carboxylic Acids, and Nitrogen Compounds.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Nitrogen Master Interconversion Web:</b><br>&bull; <b>Alkane &rarr; Nitroalkane:</b> R-H + HNO<sub>3</sub> (fuming, 673 K) &rarr; R-NO<sub>2</sub>.<br>&bull; <b>Alkyl Halide &rarr; Amine:</b> R-X + NH<sub>3</sub> &rarr; R-NH<sub>2</sub>; R-X + KCN &rarr; R-CN &rarr; (LiAlH<sub>4</sub>) &rarr; R-CH<sub>2</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub>.<br>&bull; <b>Amide &rarr; Amine:</b> R-CONH<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (LiAlH<sub>4</sub>) &rarr; R-CH<sub>2</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub> (Same carbon); R-CONH<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (Br<sub>2</sub>/NaOH) &rarr; R-NH<sub>2</sub> (1 less carbon).<br>&bull; <b>Amine &rarr; Alcohol:</b> R-NH<sub>2</sub> + HNO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; R-OH + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr;.<br>&bull; <b>Alcohol &rarr; Acid &rarr; Amide &rarr; Amine:</b> R-CH<sub>2</sub>OH &rarr; (KMnO<sub>4</sub>) &rarr; R-COOH &rarr; (NH<sub>3</sub>/&Delta;) &rarr; R-CONH<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (Br<sub>2</sub>/NaOH) &rarr; R-NH<sub>2</sub>.<br>&bull; <b>Aniline Gateway:</b> Nitrobenzene &rarr; (Fe/HCl) &rarr; Aniline &rarr; (NaNO<sub>2</sub>/HCl, 273-278 K) &rarr; Ar-N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> &rarr; Ar-Cl, Ar-Br, Ar-I, Ar-F, Ar-CN, Ar-OH, Ar-NO<sub>2</sub>, Ar-H, and Azo Dyes.</div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n";

